B_DE_160_B1_13_HSA_3.raw\nB200421_02_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_1ms_1.raw\nB200421_03_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_5ms_1.raw\nB200421_04_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_10ms_1.raw\nB200421_05_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_20ms_1.raw\nB200421_06_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_30ms_1.raw\nB200421_07_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_40ms_1.raw\nB200421_08_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_50ms_1.raw\nB200421_10_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_100ms_1.raw\nB200421_11_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_200ms_1.raw\nB200421_14_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_200ms_2.raw\nB200421_15_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_100ms_2.raw\nB200421_16_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_50ms_2.raw\nB200421_17_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_40ms_2.raw\nB200421_18_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_30ms_2.raw\nB200421_19_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_20ms_2.raw\nB200421_20_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_10ms_2.raw\nB200421_21_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_5ms_2.raw\nB200424_01_Lumos_AB_DE_165_HSA_UCCL_S1_dualHCD_UVPD_1ms_2.raw\nB201106_01_Lumos_AB_DE_145_B1_149_pD_s10_rep1.raw\nB201106_02_Lumos_AB_DE_145_B1_149_pD_s9_rep1.raw\nB201106_03_Lumos_AB_DE_145_B1_149_pD_s8_rep1.raw\nB201106_04_Lumos_AB_DE_135_B1_149_pD_s6_rep1.raw\nB201106_05_Lumos_AB_DE_135_B1_149_pD_s5_rep1.raw\nB201106_06_Lumos_AB_DE_145_B1_149_pD_s11_rep1.raw\nB201106_07_Lumos_AB_DE_145_B1_149_pD_s7_rep1.raw\nB220506_03_Lumos_LK_DE_135_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC5_rep1.raw\nB220506_04_Lumos_LK_DE_135_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC5_rep2.raw\nB220506_05_Lumos_LK_DE_135_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC5_rep3.raw\nB220506_07_Lumos_LK_DE_135_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC6_rep1.raw\nB220506_08_Lumos_LK_DE_135_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC6_rep2.raw\nB220506_09_Lumos_LK_DE_135_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC6_rep3.raw\nB220506_11_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC7_rep1.raw\nB220506_12_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC7_rep2.raw\nB220506_13_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC7_rep3.raw\nB220506_15_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC8_rep1.raw\nB220506_16_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC8_rep2.raw\nB220506_17_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC8_rep3.raw\nB220506_19_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC9_rep1.raw\nB220506_20_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC9_rep2.raw\nB220506_21_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC9_rep3.raw\nB220506_23_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC10_rep1.raw\nB220506_24_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC10_rep2.raw\nB220506_25_Lumos_LK_DE_145_UCCL_S1_HCD_UVPD_20ms_MS3_SEC10_rep3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nLight-induced orthogonal fragmentation of crosslinked peptides\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCrosslinking mass spectrometry provides pivotal information on the structure and interaction of proteins. MS-cleavable crosslinkers are regarded as a cornerstone for the analysis of complex mixtures. Yet they fragment under similar conditions as peptides, leading to mixed fragmentation spectra of the crosslinker and peptide. This hampers selecting individual peptides for their independent identification. Here we introduce orthogonal cleavage, using ultraviolet photodissociation (UVPD) to increase crosslinker over peptide fragmentation. We designed and synthesized a crosslinker that can be cleaved at 213 nm in a commercial mass spectrometer configuration. In an analysis of crosslinked E. coli lysate, the crosslinker-to-peptide fragment intensity ratio increases from nearly 1 for a conventionally cleavable crosslinker to 5 for the UVPD cleavable crosslinker. This largely increased the sensitivity of selecting the individual peptides for MS3, even more so with an improved doublet detection algorithm.\n\n[METHODS]\nProteins (purified HSA and E. coli lysate) were crosslinked using the synthesized reagent, Di(N-succinimidyl)-2,2’-[1,4-phenylenebis(methylenethio)]bis-acetate. Crosslinked proteins were precipitated in ice-cold acetone and subsequently digested overnight using trypsin along with standard published protocols. Crosslinked peptides were enriched by size-exclusion chromatography using a Superdex Peptide 3.2/300 column (GE Healthcare) with a mobile phase consisting of 30% (v/v) acetonitrile and 0.1% TFA, at a flow rate of 10 μL/min. Protein digests were analyzed using an Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano system (Dionex, Thermo Fisher Scientific, Germany) coupled on-line to an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid mass spectrometer equipped with an EasySpray source and a UVPD module (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Germany) featuring a 213 nm solid-state Nd:YAG laser head (CryLaS GmbH). Peptides were resuspended in 3.2% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid, loaded onto and eluted from a 500 mm C18 LC column (75 μm i.d., 2 μm particles, 100 Å pore size) operating at 45°C, at 300 nl/min, directly into the mass spectrometer. Mobile phase A was 0.1% formic acid and mobile phase B was 80% acetonitrile/0.1% formic acid. Peptides were analyzed using a “high-high” approach for all acquisition strategies (unless stated otherwise), with both MS1 and MS2 scans carried out in the Orbitrap. MS1 spectra were acquired at 120,000 resolution, in top-speed data-dependent mode, with monoisotopic peak determination set to “peptide”. SEC fractions from crosslinked peptide digests were sprayed directly into the mass spectrometer using gradient elution from the LC column, with gradients optimized for individual fractions (from 2-18% mobile phase B to 37.5-46.5% over 90 min, followed by a linear increase to 45-55 and 95% over 2.5 min each). Survey spectra were acquired using the following settings: Scan range 400-1600 m/z, Maximum injection time 50 ms, RF lens 35%, Minimum intensity 2.5 x 104 and Exclusion duration 60 s. Data decision tree logic was applied for MS2 scan triggering (Giese et al. 2016; Kolbowski et al. 2017), with fragmentation by stepped HCD using collision energies of 26, 28 and 30%. Precursor priority was given to the most intense ions. Ion isolation was carried out in the quadrupole with an isolation window of 1.4 m/z. MS2 scans (except where stated otherwise) were analyzed in the Orbitrap at 60,000 resolution, including charge states 3-7, scan range of 150-2000 m/z and maximum injection time set to 118 ms. Normalized AGC target was set to 200% (1 x 105). Samples were analyzed following two acquisition strategies, (1) HCD-MS2 and (2) HCD-UVPD-MS2-HCD-MS3 (MS3 scans targeting UVPD-MS2 cleaved UCCL signature peaks). In the HCD-MS2 approach, data was first generated with the purpose of creating a protein database for analyzing subsequent acquisition strategies. For this, survey spectra were acquired using a normalized AGC target of 200% (8 x 105), including charge states 2-7. MS2 scans were then analyzed in the ion trap (scan rate “normal”, maximum injection time 35 ms). Next, crosslinked peptide search-specific data was generated, acquiring survey scans with a normalized AGC target of 250% (1 x 106), including charge states 3-7. The UVPD activation time was 20 ms for the UVPD-MS2 scans. In the HCPD-UVPD-MS2-HCD-MS3 approach, the targeted mass differences for triggering HCD-MS3 scans were ΔM 103.0548, 104.0626, 105.0704 and 106.0783 Da. Dynamic exclusion was set to 2 s and mass tolerance was 8 ppm. MS3 scans were performed on the most intense ion in the pair on the condition that both ions must be of the same charge state. MS3 scans were analyzed in the ion trap using the rapid scan rate, an isolation window of 2 m/z, scan range of 150-2000, with normalised AGC target set to 100% and maximum injection time set to 35 ms. Fragmentation was achieved using HCD collision energy at 30%.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nEscherichia coli; Homo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
